-Quantile trend filtering estimates a conditional quantile along an ordered domain by combining the check loss with an $\ell_1$ penalty on the differences of order $k+1$ of the trend. The penalty must be selected, and the result is a point estimate. We propose a Bayesian counterpart: an asymmetric Laplace working likelihood and a Laplace prior on the differences, with priors on the penalty and the likelihood scale. The trend is updated by a proximal Metropolis-adjusted Langevin step whose proximal map is a weighted trend filtering problem, and the cost per sweep is linear in the sample size $n$. A joint sampler for several quantile levels produces draws that never cross. We establish that, for a fixed scale and a fixed penalty of order $n^{(2k+1)/2}$, the posterior contracts around the true quantiles at the rate $n^{-(2k+1)/(2k+2)}$, and the same rate holds at every level of the joint posterior; the data need not follow the working likelihood. In simulations at levels from $0.05$ to $0.95$, the mean squared error of the sampler is within a factor of $1.22$ of that of a horseshoe Gibbs sampler on a signal with varying smoothness; on a smooth signal, the optimization-based estimator has the smallest error. Applications to air-quality sensor data and to growth charts illustrate the method.
+Quantile trend filtering estimates a conditional quantile along an ordered domain by combining the check loss with an $\ell_1$ penalty on the differences of order $k+1$ of the trend. The penalty must be selected, and the result is a point estimate. We propose a Bayesian counterpart: an asymmetric Laplace working likelihood and a Laplace prior on the differences, with priors on the penalty and the likelihood scale. The trend is updated by a proximal Metropolis-adjusted Langevin step whose proximal map is a weighted trend filtering problem, and the cost per sweep is linear in the sample size $n$. A joint sampler for several quantile levels produces draws that never cross. We establish that, for a fixed scale and a fixed penalty of order $n^{(2k+1)/2}$, the posterior contracts around the true quantiles at the rate $n^{-(2k+1)/(2k+2)}$, and the same rate holds at every level of the joint posterior; the data need not follow the working likelihood. In simulations at levels from $0.05$ to $0.95$, the mean squared error of the sampler is within a factor of $1.37$ of that of a horseshoe Gibbs sampler on a signal with varying smoothness; on a smooth signal, the optimization-based estimator has the smallest error. Applications to air-quality sensor data and to growth charts illustrate the method.
 
 % Abstract before the W-04 rewrite (2026-11-24), kept for reference:
 % Quantile trend filtering combines the check loss with an $\ell_1$ penalty on discrete differences of the trend. We develop Bayesian-PMALA, a posterior sampler for quantile trend filtering that learns the regularization strength and the likelihood scale jointly with the trend. Augmenting the asymmetric Laplace likelihood with its normal--exponential representation makes the likelihood Gaussian in the trend. Under a global Laplace prior on trend differences, the whole negative log-conditional of the trend is then a weighted quadratic plus an $\ell_1$ difference penalty. The trend can therefore be updated by an exact proximal Metropolis-adjusted Langevin (P-MALA) step. The Moreau--Yosida envelope is built on the entire conditional posterior, through a weighted trend filtering problem, rather than on a smoothed check loss, and the Metropolis--Hastings correction uses the exact density. The latent variables, the penalty and the scale have conjugate updates. Every linear system is banded, and the proximal map is computed by ADMM with a factorization shared by the forward and reverse maps, so the work per sweep grows linearly with the sample size for a fixed inner iteration budget. For several quantile levels, a joint version samples each trend between its neighbours. Only the proximal map changes, becoming a box-constrained problem solved with banded interior-point steps, and no retained draw crosses.
