 \paragraph{Curvature-matched metric.} With $M = \mathbf{I}_n$ the noise in \eqref{eq:pmala_proposal} is white, and its $(k+1)$-th differences have standard deviation of order $\sqrt{\delta}$ in each of the $m$ coordinates, each charged by the penalty at rate $\lambda$. For the posterior values of $\lambda$ at $n = 500$ this increases $U$ by far more than the Metropolis--Hastings ratio can absorb: on a pilot configuration (VS signal, Gaussian noise, $\tau = 0.5$) the acceptance rate was zero for every step size tried down to $\delta = 10^{-5}$. The metric \eqref{eq:metric} scales the noise in the rough directions by $1/\lambda$. Values of $\kappa$ between $3$ and $10$ raised the adapted step size three- to tenfold relative to $\kappa = 1$ at the same acceptance rate and increased the effective sample size of $\theta$ several-fold; the default is $\kappa = 3$.
 
-\paragraph{Rate of the Gamma prior.} Because the posterior draws of the trend satisfy $\norm{D\theta}_1 \approx m/\lambda$ (Appendix~\ref{app:pmala:asis}), the data determine the level of $\lambda$ only weakly, and the prior rate $b_0$ must be small relative to $\norm{D\theta}_1$ for the data to determine $\lambda$. A unit rate is far from weak on this scale: $\mathrm{Gamma}(1,1)$ gives $P(\lambda > 100) = e^{-100}$, whereas $\mathrm{Gamma}(1, 10^{-2})$ gives $P(\lambda > 100) = e^{-1} \approx 0.37$. Under $\mathrm{Gamma}(1,1)$ the chain settled at $\lambda$ between $50$ and $75$ on the pilot configuration and returned undersmoothed trends. We therefore use $b_0 = 10^{-2}$. A sensitivity run with $b_0 = 10^{-4}$ on all $30$ configurations with $10$ replications each was not uniformly better: on the smooth signal the geometric-mean MSE ratio to the default was $0.86$, with $7$ of $15$ configurations significantly lower and none higher, but the median coverage of the $95\%$ intervals fell from $0.91$ to $0.84$; on the varying-smoothness signal the ratio was $1.01$, with $1$ configuration significantly lower and $2$ higher. The default therefore remains $b_0 = 10^{-2}$. The interior-quantile simulations of Section~\ref{sec:sim:individual} use the smaller rates $b_0 = 10^{-3}$ for the varying-smoothness signal and $b_0 = 5\times10^{-4}$ for the smooth signal. A Laplace prior scaled by $\sigma$ \citep{park2008bayesian}, under which the penalty enters as $\lambda/\sigma$, is not used: in one configuration $\lambda$ and $\sigma$ escalated together to very large values for a long stretch of the chain, a mode the unscaled prior \eqref{eq:prior_theta} does not exhibit.
