+The competitors are the Laplace and horseshoe Gibbs samplers of \citet{onizuka2024fast} and the ADMM estimator of \citet{brantley2020baseline}. The levels are $\tau=0.05$, $0.25$, $0.50$, $0.75$, and $0.95$, and all methods are fitted to the same data in each replication. Bayesian-PMALA uses $\sigma\sim\mathrm{IG}(0.1,0.1)$, $\lambda\sim\mathrm{Gamma}(1,10^{-5})$ for both signals, and one chain of $30000$ sweeps per fit, of which the first $10000$ are discarded. For these interior levels the sampler of Section~\ref{subsec:individual} is run with three updates, stated in Appendix~\ref{app:pmala:interior}: the chain starts from a running-median fit of the data rather than from $\by$, $\lambda$ is held at its initial value during the warm-up, and the interweaving step keeps a spline component of the trend fixed, rather than only its polynomial component, while it rescales the remainder. All other results in the paper use the sampler as described in Section~\ref{sec:methodology} and Appendix~\ref{app:pmala}. The samplers of \citet{onizuka2024fast} use $8000$ sweeps with $2000$ burn-in and an $\mathrm{IG}(1,1)$ prior on $\sigma^2$. The Bayesian point estimates are posterior means. ADMM selects $\lambda$ by extended BIC over a $25$-point logarithmic grid, and a replication is discarded when any candidate on the grid fails to converge.
 
 For each fit we compute $\mathrm{MSE}=n^{-1}\sum_i(\widehat\theta_{\tau,i}-Q_\tau(u_i))^2$, $\mathrm{MAD}=n^{-1}\sum_i|\widehat\theta_{\tau,i}-Q_\tau(u_i)|$, and, for the samplers, the mean width MCIW of the pointwise $95\%$ intervals. All three are averaged over valid replications.
 
-\label{sec:sim:ind:vs}Table~\ref{tab:vs_int_mse} reports the MSE for the VS signal; MAD and MCIW are in Appendix~\ref{app:sim:individual:vs}. In every configuration the MSE values of the three Bayesian samplers are within a factor of $1.22$ of one another, and the horseshoe has the smallest at all five levels under Beta noise. A single global penalty therefore gives nearly the same error as local shrinkage on this signal. ADMM has the largest MSE in $14$ of the $15$ configurations; the exception is mixed-normal noise at $\tau=0.95$. This is consistent with extended BIC selecting a penalty too large for the localized bump, whereas the samplers estimate a smaller one.
+\label{sec:sim:ind:vs}Table~\ref{tab:vs_int_mse} reports the MSE for the VS signal; MAD and MCIW are in Appendix~\ref{app:sim:individual:vs}. In every configuration the MSE values of the three Bayesian samplers are within a factor of $1.37$ of one another, and the horseshoe has the smallest at all five levels under Beta noise. The largest differences are under mixed-normal noise at $\tau=0.25$, $0.50$, and $0.75$, where Bayesian-PMALA has the largest MSE of the three samplers, by $7\%$ to $37\%$ relative to the better of the other two. In the other configurations the three are within a factor of $1.21$ of one another, so there a single global penalty gives nearly the same error as local shrinkage. ADMM has the largest MSE in $14$ of the $15$ configurations; the exception is mixed-normal noise at $\tau=0.95$. This is consistent with extended BIC selecting a penalty too large for the localized bump, whereas the samplers estimate a smaller one.
 
