-\label{sec:sim:ind:smooth}Table~\ref{tab:s_int_mse} reports the MSE for the S signal; MAD and MCIW are in Appendix~\ref{app:sim:individual:smooth}. ADMM has the smallest MSE in every configuration; the MSE of Bayesian-PMALA is between $1.3$ and $2.0$ times as large. This is the smooth-baseline setting for which ADMM was developed. Bayesian-PMALA has a smaller MSE than both samplers of \citet{onizuka2024fast} in every configuration. Relative to the better of the two, its MSE is $25\%$ to $43\%$ lower at $\tau=0.25$, $0.50$, and $0.75$, and $4\%$ to $33\%$ lower at $\tau=0.05$ and $0.95$. This signal has no localized feature to which local shrinkage scales could adapt.
